# 619 ERP Frontend — env template
# Copy to .env.local and fill in real values.
# NEVER commit .env.local (it's in .gitignore).

# Backend API URL (Render deployment or local)
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://six19-erp-api.onrender.com

# Node environment (auto-set by Next.js in production)
# NODE_ENV=production

# JWT secret — OPTIONAL, and recommended to LEAVE UNSET.
#
# Setting it copies the backend's symmetric signing key into this deployment,
# so anyone who can read the frontend's environment can mint valid admin
# tokens. The only thing it buys is that src/proxy.ts verifies the signature
# instead of checking expiry alone — and the proxy is a UX gate, not the
# security boundary: the backend verifies the signature on every single API
# call regardless. Unset, an unexpired forged token gets you a page shell and
# 401s from every request behind it.
#
# If you do want signature verification at the edge without sharing the key,
# move the backend to RS256 and put only the PUBLIC key here.
# JWT_SECRET=<same-value-as-backend-JWT_SECRET>

# NOT USED by this app — do not add them:
#   NEXTAUTH_SECRET / NEXTAUTH_URL — there is no next-auth dependency; auth is
#     the backend's own JWT in an httpOnly cookie.
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET — the frontend uses the popup flow and needs only the
#     public client id below. The secret belongs to the backend, which performs
#     the code exchange (see backend src/lib/google-calendar.js).
# Anything in that list sitting in this deployment's environment is a secret
# with no purpose and a real blast radius.

# Google OAuth — enables the "Sign in with Google" button on the login page
# Get from Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth 2.0 Client ID
# Set Authorised JavaScript origins: https://your-domain.com
# Set Authorised redirect URIs: https://your-domain.com (no callback path needed — we use popup flow)
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=<your-google-oauth-client-id>.apps.googleusercontent.com

# NOTE: WebAuthn/passkey config (RP_ID, WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN, RP_NAME) lives
# entirely on the backend — see 619-erp-backend/.env.example. The frontend
# has no code path that reads a WEBAUTHN_* variable.

# Supabase image hostname for Next.js image optimizer (M-02)
# Find in Supabase Dashboard → Settings → API → Project URL (hostname only)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_HOST=<your-project-ref>.supabase.co

# Full Supabase project URL — used by src/proxy.ts to build the CSP's
# connect-src allowlist. Same project as NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_HOST above,
# just the full URL instead of the bare hostname; both are required.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<your-project-ref>.supabase.co

# ── Sentry (client-side error monitoring, optional) ──────────────────
# Inert unless set.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://<key>@<org>.ingest.sentry.io/<project>
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATE=0.1

# ── Branding (white-label — set per client deployment) ───────────────
# Shown as the support contact across the app. If unset, falls back to
# the original vendor's number — always set this for a new client.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_PHONE=+91XXXXXXXXXX

# ── CSP violation reporting (optional) ────────────────────────────────
# The strict, nonce-based Content-Security-Policy ships as Report-Only
# alongside the enforced one (see src/lib/security-headers.js). It breaks
# nothing; it only reports what WOULD be blocked if 'unsafe-inline' were
# removed from script-src. Leave unset and violations appear only in the
# browser console. Set it to collect them centrally before flipping the
# policy to enforced. Sentry accepts CSP reports at:
#   https://<org>.ingest.sentry.io/api/<project-id>/security/?sentry_key=<public-key>
# CSP_REPORT_URI=

# ── Command Center hostname (platform control plane) ──────────────────
# The owner's console (/platform) is a separate portal from the studio
# app: its own route group, its own shell, its own sign-in door
# (/platform-login), and — with this set — its own hostname.
#
# COMMAND_CENTER_HOST=admin.myptstudio.com
#
# With it set, src/proxy.ts serves the console ONLY on that host and 404s
# /platform and /platform-login on the studio's domain. A tenant admin
# poking at /platform gets the same answer as for a URL that was never
# built — nothing about the console's existence or shape is observable
# from the customer's domain.
#
# It runs the other way too: studio pages 404 on the Command Center host,
# so the operator's domain does not quietly become a second front door to
# the customer app on a hostname the cookie and CSP config never expected.
#
# NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_. It is read server-side, in the edge proxy, and must
# not be shipped to the browser: the whole point is that the console's
# address is not in a bundle every tenant downloads.
#
# Unset (the default) applies no host rule, which is what local
# development and single-domain deployments need. In production it pairs
# with the admin.myptstudio.com vhost in the backend repo's
# infra/nginx/myptstudio.conf, and with PLATFORM_SESSION_ENFORCE on the
# API — the hostname is the outer boundary, the session audience is the
# one that actually refuses a request.

# ── Studio app origin, as the Command Center should address it ────────
# Only needed when COMMAND_CENTER_HOST above is set, i.e. when the console
# and the studio app are on different hostnames.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ORIGIN=https://myptstudio.com
#
# It is what "Enter this studio" in the console navigates to. Without it
# the console sends the operator to its OWN '/', which on a split
# deployment is a 404 — the console's host serves the console and nothing
# else.
#
# It also decides how the impersonation token crosses over. sessionStorage
# is scoped to an origin, so on a split deployment the token is handed to
# the studio app in the URL fragment (see consumeHandoff in src/lib/http.ts).
# A fragment is never sent to a server, which is the only reason that is an
# acceptable place to put a live token for one navigation.
#
# Public by design, unlike COMMAND_CENTER_HOST: this is the customer-facing
# address that every tenant already types. Leave unset for single-origin
# deployments, where same-origin navigation and sessionStorage both work.
